---
description: Apply when asked to simplify or clean up code, or when sweeping a diff before a PR
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- Declutter is a cleanup-only pass: it applies behavior-preserving simplifications and nothing else — no bug fixes, no redesign. Run the tests first and record the result; red suite → stop; no coverage → only zero-risk subtractions (dead code, unused imports, debug leftovers).
- Scope: the current diff (commits ahead of upstream + uncommitted changes) — cleaning untouched code turns a reviewable diff into archaeology; note pre-existing clutter for a separate pass.
- Sweep in value order: duplication of what already exists (search before believing new code is new) · dead weight (unreachable branches, unused vars/imports, commented-out code, debug prints, done TODOs) · speculative abstraction (one-implementation interfaces, forwarding layers) · needless indirection · simpler equivalents (guard clauses, stdlib idioms, the boring two lines over the clever one) · free inefficiency fixes only when also cleaner — measurement-needing work is performance work, not cleanup.
- Found a bug while sweeping? Do NOT fix it here — report it (`SEVERITY [CONFIRMED|PLAUSIBLE] path:line — defect — trigger — fix`). Behavior changes hidden inside cleanup diffs are the most dangerous kind.
- Don't touch: public API surface (removing an unused public export is breaking — flag it), commented workarounds, performance-shaped code. Unsure if load-bearing → check callers and git history; still unsure → leave it, note it.
- Prove: full suite after equals before, both quoted. Never golf (fewer characters ≠ simpler), never restyle against the file's idiom, never trust "obviously equivalent" without the test run.
